```csharp
[My("hello\0")]
class MyAttribute : Attribute
{
    public MyAttribute(params object[] arr)
    {
    }
}
```

Reading the above with System.Reflection.Metadata's CustomAttribute.DecodeValue claims that the argument to My is `hello` instead of `hello\0`.

I didn't debug into it, but from source code inspection this is caused by the following (Roslyn specific?) compat quirk:

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/79ae74f5ca5c8a6fe3a48935e85bd7374959c570/src/libraries/System.Reflection.Metadata/src/System/Reflection/Metadata/BlobReader.cs#L594-L596

Would be nice to be able to opt out of this.

System.Reflection.Metadata is currently unable to decode a lot of custom attributes in this very repo because putting a `\0` is popular in various InlineDatas:

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/128c757a3ac580829d08c2a60aa205559ccde8f9/src/libraries/Common/tests/Tests/System/StringTests.cs#L78

